The general approach in cut optimization is to maximize the background rejection for a given signal efficiency and scan the full range of the latter quantity. Signal efficiency \(\epsilon_{S}\) is given by:

\[\epsilon_{S}=\frac{N_{J/\psi}^{surv}}{N_{J/\psi}^{Init}} \tag{3.19}\]

where: \(N_{J/\psi}^{Init}\) is initial number of \(J/\psi\) mesons in the signal model and \(N_{J/\psi}^{surv}\) is number of \(J/\psi\) surviving kinematic and PID cuts.

So far there is no reliable Monte Carlo simulations for the dE/dx in TPC, therefore the real data have to be used in particle identification assessment. We incorporated PID efficiency in \(N_{J/\psi}^{surv}\) evaluation using formula:

\[N_{J/\psi}^{surv}=N_{J/\psi}^{kin.cuts}\cdot\epsilon_{PID}(p^{e+})\cdot \epsilon_{PID}(p^{e-}) \tag{3.20}\]

where: \(N_{J/\psi}^{kin.cuts}\) - number of \(J/\psi\)'s surviving kinematic cuts, \(\epsilon_{PID}(p^{e})\) - single electron identification efficiency for a given momentum \(p\). The electron identification efficiency was estimated using procedure described in section 3.5.

Background efficiency \(\epsilon_{B}\) is given by:

\[\epsilon_{B}=\frac{N_{B}^{surv}}{N_{B}^{Init}} \tag{3.21}\]

where: \(N_{B}^{Init}\) is initial number of background pairs and \(N_{B}^{surv}\) is number of background pairs surviving kinematic and PID cuts.

An Analysis in which a significance is maximized requires knowledge of expected signal, \(N_{S}\), and background,\(N_{B}\), yields for a given set of cuts used as starting point in studies, i.e. absolute normalization is necessary. Then the signal significance can be calculated using:

\[Sig=\frac{N_{S}\epsilon_{S}}{\sqrt{N_{S}\epsilon_{S}+N_{B}\epsilon_{B}}} \tag{3.22}\]

The statistical uncertainty on the significance was estimated using error propagation formula:

\[\Delta Sig=|\frac{\delta Sig}{\delta S}|\Delta S+|\frac{\delta Sig}{\delta B} |\Delta B \tag{3.23}\]

Where: \(\Delta S=\sqrt{N_{S}\epsilon_{S}}\) and \(\Delta B=\sqrt{B_{S}\epsilon_{B}}\).

It is natural to start optimization with set of loose cuts to have as much signal as possible in the initial sample. For relaxed selection criteria the normalization for background can be easily obtained with satisfactory precision using like-sign method. However, \(J/\psi\) peak in such case sits on top of large combinational background and \(J/\psi\) yield can be extracted only with \(20-30\%\) precision for data analyzed here. This normalization error is the main systematic uncertainty of these studies. The second source of systematic error is PID efficiency \(\epsilon_{PID}\) - it was calculated for a given initial set of kinematic and PID cuts and then used as a electron selection probability with assumption that \(\epsilon_{PID}\) does not change significantly for different set of kinematic cuts. Although \(\epsilon_{PID}\) may change slightly with \(p_{T}\) cut, the \(\epsilon_{PID}\) assessment is a very complicated process and it was not feasible to calculate \(\epsilon_{PID}\) for every \(p_{T}\) cut examined in the studies.

