The background model was constructed using the real data, combinatorial background was estimated by sum of like-sign pairs with \(2.8<M_{inv}<3.2\) GeV/c\({}^{2}\). Such method is equivalent to geometrical mean and it was easier to implement in the TMVA environment. It is also less computationally expensive, which is especially important when the rectangular cut optimization is employed. Cuts used to select background pairs are listed in Tab. 3.6 The sample consisted of 19 thousand like-sign pairs for Cu+Cu collisions and 7 thousand pairs for Au+Au.

