## Chapter 5 Nuclear modification factor study

\(J/\psi\) is considered to be an excellent probe to study the properties of Quark-Gluon Plasma. To study the \(J/\psi\) interaction with a medium, charmonium production in nucleus+nucleus collisions is compared to the production in \(p+p\). Then, using models or theoretical calculations, we try to deduce the properties of the medium based on the modification of the properties of the probe, \(J/\psi\) in this case.

The modification of particle production in the medium is quantified by the nuclear modification factor \(R_{AA}\), which, as a function of \(p_{T}\), is given by the ratio of particle production in nucleus-nucleus collisions \(\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{A+A}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\right)\) in a given centrality class, to the production rate in elementary (proton-proton) collisions \(\left(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\right)\), scaled by the mean number of binary collisions in the reaction \(N_{coll}\):

\[R_{AA}(p_{T})=\frac{\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{A+A}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}}{ \left<N_{coll}\right>\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}} \tag{5.1}\]

The \(J/\psi\) production in \(p+p\) collisions is reported in literature as a differential cross-section \(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\sigma_{p+p}^{J/\psi}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\), and it allow us to obtain the invariant yield \(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\) as follows:

\[\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}N^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}=\frac{\frac{\mathrm{ d}^{2}\sigma_{p+p}^{J/\psi}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}}{\frac{\mathrm{d}y \mathrm{d}p_{T}}{\sigma_{p+p}}} \tag{5.2}\]

where \(\sigma_{p+p}=42\pm 3\) mb is the cross-section of inelastic \(p+p\) collisions.

\(R_{AA}\) is also used to quantify the in-medium modification of the production rates as a function of centrality of the collision, represented by the number of participants, \(N_{part}\). For a given colliding system and a colliding energy, the centrality dependence reflects an evolution of \(R_{AA}\) with energy density. \(R_{AA}\) for a given centrality class, corresponding to a particular number ofbinary collisions and participants, is given by:

\[R_{AA}=\frac{\frac{\mathrm{d}N^{A+A}}{\mathrm{d}y}}{\langle N_{coll}\rangle\ \frac{\mathrm{d}N^{p+p}}{\mathrm{d}y}} \tag{5.3}\]

The number of binary collisions and the number of participants were calculated within the framework of a Monte Carlo Glauber model and are presented in Tab 5.1 and 5.2 for Au+Au and Cu+Cu centrality classes respectively.

In our studies we used the \(J/\psi\) differential cross sections \(\frac{\mathrm{d}\sigma_{p+p}^{J/\psi}}{\mathrm{d}y}\) and \(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\sigma_{p+p}^{J/\psi}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\) reported by the STAR experiment [89] to calculate \(R_{AA}\). For the sake of comparison, we also used \(\frac{\mathrm{d}^{2}\sigma_{p+p}^{J/\psi}}{\mathrm{d}y\mathrm{d}p_{T}}\) from PHENIX [110] to calculate \(R_{AA}(p_{T})\).

